body:
Updates the existing `mathgraph` checker to the next retained MathGraph revision.

The first public MathGraph entry is pinned at `1e209dad266d59b4d43cdea189876bc6ea551339`. This revision starts from that exact checker state and adds one intervention only: a fixed-stride 32-entry jump pointer for deep persistent `Env::Cons` lookup.

The checker revision is pinned at `metalogiclabs/mathgraph-lean-kernel@af4a9432ac432df1dc896032294454ff31a7be6f`. The build applies the checked-in `scripts/apply_jump32.py` transformation before the unchanged MathGraph PGO recipe.

Pre-submission validation of the same intervention:

- 38/38 sokonanoda unit tests passed in the experimental runs;
- full supported Arena corpus completed for both public MathGraph and jump32 with identical semantic totals: 183 correct + 6 either;
- the private full-corpus wall-time comparison was ~5.5% faster overall for jump32 on that runner;
- the deep `beta-ladder` residual showed a large retained gain, while broader controls were used to reject more complex alternatives.

The purpose of this PR is to let the Arena's official runner provide the decisive instruction-count measurement. If the official result does not improve the existing MathGraph entry, the intervention should not be treated as a retained leaderboard gain.

Adds `mathgraph` as a separate Arena checker while preserving the existing upstream `sokonanoda` entry unchanged.

MathGraph is a verifier-preserving optimization derived from `intgrah/sokonanoda@0fab8874080e379a774a9a27f7538d8a1ddd786b` and maintained in the Metalogic Labs fork at `metalogiclabs/mathgraph-lean-kernel`.

The fork differs from that upstream revision in only two Rust source edits:

- increase `SESSION_BUDGET` from `1 << 20` to `2_621_440`;
- in the inner Lambda evaluation loop, extend the current environment directly instead of recomputing `key_env` before each extension.

Validation before adding the separate checker entry:

- `cargo test --locked`: 38/38 unit tests passed;
- Arena fast-tier test passed;
- release build passed;
- full supported Arena corpus completed successfully from the real fork: all 183 scored cases correct, plus 6 `either` cases;
- the canonical Arena PR workflow completed successfully under the final `mathgraph` identity.

The public checker identity is simply `mathgraph`; there is no experimental `A3` label in the Arena-facing configuration. The checker description links both the upstream `sokonanoda` implementation lineage and https://mathgraph.org/.

This is the first retained MathGraph optimization iteration intended for Arena. Further iterations will keep the same verifier-first discipline: measure the remaining residuals, test minimal refinements, and update the checker only when a change survives the semantic gates.

Two tests for the same lie of claiming a recursor is K-like when it is not

## rec-k-lie

The schematic version of the code looks like

```lean
/-- Lie: MyBool.rec is K-like -/
inductive MyBool | false | true

def disc : MyBool → Prop := fun b => MyBool.rec True False b

theorem bad : disc MyBool.true := True.intro
```

Metaprogramming to generate a bogus export:

```lean
module

public import Lean
import all Lean.Environment

open Lean Elab Command

public inductive MyBool | false | true

public noncomputable def disc : MyBool → Prop := fun b => MyBool.rec True False b

run_cmd liftTermElabM do
  let .recInfo rv ← getConstInfo ``MyBool.rec | throwError "MyBool.rec is not a recursor"
  let ci : ConstantInfo := .recInfo { rv with k := true }
  modifyEnv fun env => { env with
    base.public.constants.map₁ := env.base.public.constants.map₁.insert ci.name ci
    base.private.constants.map₁ := env.base.private.constants.map₁.insert ci.name ci
    checked := env.checked.map fun e => { e with constants := e.constants.insert ci.name ci }
  }

public theorem bad : disc MyBool.true := True.intro
```

